LESSON 4: SETTING UP LOADING STATE
================================================================================
PART 1: LESSON OVERVIEW
================================================================================

This lesson teaches you how to implement a loading state in React to provide
clear user feedback while data is being fetched from a backend API.

Instead of showing a blank screen while waiting for the server, the application
can display:

Loading...

This makes the application easier to understand and improves the overall user
experience.


WHAT YOU'LL LEARN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• What a loading state is

• Why loading feedback is important

• How to create loading state with useState

• How to start loading before an API request

• How to stop loading after the request completes

• How to handle loading during errors

• How to use loading state with useEffect

• How to conditionally display Loading, Error, and Data states


================================================================================
PART 2: WHY DO WE NEED A LOADING STATE?
================================================================================

API calls are asynchronous.

When React sends a request to a backend:

React Application
       ↓
Send API Request
       ↓
Backend Processes Request
       ↓
Wait for Response
       ↓
Response Arrives
       ↓
Update React State
       ↓
Display Data


The response may take:

100 ms

500 ms

2 seconds

5 seconds

or longer.


Without a loading indicator, users may see:

Blank Screen
     ↓
No Feedback
     ↓
User Thinks:
"Is the application broken?"


With loading state:

API Request
     ↓
Loading...
     ↓
Response Arrives
     ↓
Display Data


================================================================================
WHY LOADING STATE IS IMPORTANT
================================================================================

• API calls take time

  You don't know exactly how long the backend will take to respond.


• Better user experience

  Users receive immediate feedback instead of seeing a blank screen.


• Clear communication

  The application tells the user that something is happening.


• Professional appearance

  Loading indicators make applications feel more polished and responsive.


• Slow networks

  Loading feedback becomes especially important when network connectivity
  is slow.


• Large datasets

  Backend processing or transferring a large response may take additional
  time.


================================================================================
PART 3: THE PROBLEM WE'RE SOLVING
================================================================================

+--------------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| SCENARIO                 | WITHOUT LOADING STATE    | WITH LOADING STATE       |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| User requests data       | Blank screen             | "Loading..." displayed   |
|                          |                          |                          |
| Data is being fetched    | No clear feedback        | User knows request is    |
|                          |                          | processing               |
|                          |                          |                          |
| Network is slow          | User may think app       | Loading indicator        |
|                          | is broken                | remains visible          |
|                          |                          |                          |
| Data finally arrives     | Data suddenly appears    | Loading transitions      |
|                          |                          | to actual data           |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------+


CORE IDEA:

REQUEST STARTS
     ↓
isLoading = true
     ↓
SHOW LOADING MESSAGE
     ↓
REQUEST FINISHES
     ↓
isLoading = false
     ↓
SHOW DATA / ERROR


================================================================================
PART 4: CREATING THE LOADING STATE
================================================================================

Import React Hooks:

import {
  useEffect,
  useState
} from 'react'


Create component state:

function MyComponent() {

  const [
    data,
    setData
  ] = useState(null)


  const [
    isLoading,
    setIsLoading
  ] = useState(false)


  // Rest of component...

}


================================================================================
LOADING STATE EXPLANATION
================================================================================

+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
| CODE                        | EXPLANATION                                |
+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
| isLoading                   | Current loading status                     |
| setIsLoading                | Function used to change loading status     |
| useState(false)             | Initially no request is running            |
| setIsLoading(true)          | Request has started                        |
| setIsLoading(false)         | Request has completed                      |
+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------+


BOOLEAN MEANING:

isLoading = true

means:

REQUEST IS IN PROGRESS


isLoading = false

means:

REQUEST IS NOT CURRENTLY IN PROGRESS


================================================================================
PART 5: CHOOSING THE INITIAL LOADING VALUE
================================================================================

The correct initial value depends on how the component works.


OPTION 1: REQUEST STARTS AFTER USER ACTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

User clicks:

Search


Then:

const [
  isLoading,
  setIsLoading
] = useState(false)


Initially:

isLoading = false


After user clicks Search:

isLoading = true


This makes sense because there is no request during the initial render.


================================================================================
OPTION 2: REQUEST STARTS IMMEDIATELY
================================================================================

If the component automatically fetches data when it loads:

useEffect(() => {

  fetchData()

}, [])


then initializing loading to:

true


can be clearer:

const [
  isLoading,
  setIsLoading
] = useState(true)


Initial UI:

Loading...


instead of temporarily showing an empty state before the request starts.


MEMORY:

INITIAL isLoading VALUE
SHOULD MATCH THE INITIAL REQUEST STATE.


================================================================================
PART 6: STARTING THE LOADING STATE
================================================================================

Before calling the backend:

setIsLoading(
  true
)


Example:

const getData =
  async () => {

    setIsLoading(
      true
    )


    const response =
      await fetch(
        '/api/books'
      )

  }


FLOW:

getData()
   ↓
setIsLoading(true)
   ↓
React Re-renders
   ↓
Loading... displayed
   ↓
API Request


================================================================================
PART 7: COMPLETE LOADING STATE EXAMPLE
================================================================================

File:

src/components/BookComponent.jsx


import {
  useEffect,
  useState
} from 'react'


export default function BookComponent() {

  // ============================================
  // STATE
  // ============================================

  const [
    books,
    setBooks
  ] = useState([])


  const [
    isLoading,
    setIsLoading
  ] = useState(true)


  const [
    error,
    setError
  ] = useState(null)


  // ============================================
  // EFFECT
  // ============================================

  useEffect(() => {

    const controller =
      new AbortController()


    const getData =
      async () => {

        try {

          // ====================================
          // START LOADING
          // ====================================

          setIsLoading(
            true
          )


          setError(
            null
          )


          // ====================================
          // FETCH DATA
          // ====================================

          const response =
            await fetch(
              '/api/books',
              {
                signal:
                  controller.signal
              }
            )


          // ====================================
          // CHECK HTTP RESPONSE
          // ====================================

          if (!response.ok) {

            throw new Error(
              `Failed to load books: ${response.status}`
            )
          }


          // ====================================
          // PARSE RESPONSE
          // ====================================

          const result =
            await response.json()


          // ====================================
          // STORE DATA
          // ====================================

          setBooks(
            result
          )


        } catch (error) {

          // Ignore intentional cancellation.

          if (
            error.name ===
            'AbortError'
          ) {

            return
          }


          // ====================================
          // HANDLE ERROR
          // ====================================

          setError(
            error instanceof Error
              ? error.message
              : 'Failed to load books.'
          )


        } finally {

          // ====================================
          // STOP LOADING
          // ====================================

          if (
            !controller.signal.aborted
          ) {

            setIsLoading(
              false
            )
          }

        }
      }


    getData()


    // ==========================================
    // CLEANUP
    // ==========================================

    return () => {

      controller.abort()

    }

  }, [])


  // ============================================
  // LOADING STATE
  // ============================================

  if (isLoading) {

    return (

      <p role="status">

        Loading books...

      </p>

    )
  }


  // ============================================
  // ERROR STATE
  // ============================================

  if (error) {

    return (

      <p role="alert">

        Error:
        {' '}
        {error}

      </p>

    )
  }


  // ============================================
  // EMPTY STATE
  // ============================================

  if (books.length === 0) {

    return (

      <p>

        No books found.

      </p>

    )
  }


  // ============================================
  // SUCCESS STATE
  // ============================================

  return (

    <div className="book-list">

      {books.map(
        book => (

          <div
            key={book.id}
            className="book-card"
          >

            <h3>

              {book.title}

            </h3>


            <p>

              {book.author}

            </p>

          </div>

        )
      )}

    </div>

  )
}


================================================================================
PART 8: CODE BREAKDOWN
================================================================================

+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| CODE                     | WHAT IT DOES                                 |
+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------+
| books                    | Stores fetched book data                     |
| setBooks                 | Updates book data                            |
| isLoading                | Stores request loading status                |
| setIsLoading             | Changes loading status                       |
| error                    | Stores request failure                       |
| setError                 | Updates error state                          |
| setIsLoading(true)       | Indicates request has started                |
| fetch('/api/books')      | Calls backend API                            |
| response.ok              | Checks HTTP request result                   |
| response.json()          | Converts response body to JavaScript data    |
| setBooks(result)         | Stores successful response                   |
| catch                    | Handles request failures                     |
| finally                  | Runs after success or failure                 |
| setIsLoading(false)      | Marks request as completed                   |
| AbortController          | Cancels obsolete request                     |
+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 9: THE THREE KEY LOADING POINTS
================================================================================

1. BEFORE FETCHING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Set:

isLoading = true


Code:

setIsLoading(
  true
)


Why?

The application needs to tell the user:

THE REQUEST HAS STARTED.


FLOW:

Request Begins
     ↓
isLoading = true
     ↓
Loading UI


================================================================================
2. AFTER DATA ARRIVES
================================================================================

When the backend responds:

const result =
  await response.json()


Store the response:

setBooks(
  result
)


The request eventually finishes:

isLoading = false


Then:

Loading UI
     ↓
Book Data


================================================================================
3. AFTER AN ERROR
================================================================================

The loading indicator must also stop if the request fails.


Example:

catch (error) {

  setError(
    error.message
  )

}


The failure path must eventually result in:

isLoading = false


Otherwise:

Request Fails
     ↓
Loading remains true
     ↓
Loading...
Loading...
Loading...


The user never sees the real error.


================================================================================
PART 10: WHY finally IS USEFUL
================================================================================

Without finally:

try {

  const response =
    await fetch(
      '/api/books'
    )


  const result =
    await response.json()


  setBooks(
    result
  )


  setIsLoading(
    false
  )


} catch (error) {

  setError(
    error.message
  )


  setIsLoading(
    false
  )

}


Notice:

setIsLoading(false)


is duplicated.


================================================================================
WITH finally
================================================================================

try {

  // API request


} catch (error) {

  // Error handling


} finally {

  setIsLoading(
    false
  )

}


Now the shared request completion logic exists in one place.


FLOW:

          SUCCESS
             \
              \
               ↓
             finally
               ↓
      setIsLoading(false)
               ↑
              /
             /
           ERROR


================================================================================
CANCELLATION VERSION
================================================================================

When using AbortController:

finally {

  if (
    !controller.signal.aborted
  ) {

    setIsLoading(
      false
    )
  }

}


This prevents an obsolete aborted request from incorrectly controlling the
loading status for a newer request.


================================================================================
PART 11: CONDITIONAL RENDERING
================================================================================

Loading state becomes useful when it controls what the user sees.


================================================================================
PATTERN 1: EARLY RETURN
================================================================================

if (isLoading) {

  return (

    <p>
      Loading...
    </p>

  )
}


if (error) {

  return (

    <p>
      Error:
      {error}
    </p>

  )
}


return (

  <Content />

)


This is often the easiest pattern to read.


================================================================================
PATTERN 2: TERNARY
================================================================================

return (

  isLoading

    ? <Loading />

    : <Content />

)


Use this for simple two-state rendering.


================================================================================
PATTERN 3: MULTIPLE UI STATES
================================================================================

if (isLoading) {

  return (
    <Loading />
  )
}


if (error) {

  return (
    <ErrorMessage />
  )
}


if (books.length === 0) {

  return (
    <EmptyState />
  )
}


return (
  <BookList
    books={books}
  />
)


This gives us:

LOADING

ERROR

EMPTY

SUCCESS


